      "math_academy_id": "q-51419",
      "sequence_position": 13,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/472b1c50cef2f5ce7be11fc83eda45775e734125ba0572305ab828dc7ed278b4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice the following: \n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. Also:\n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave down. So, ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing on this interval.\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is a increasing straight line. So, $y={f}^{′}(x)$ will be a positive constant on this interval.\n\nAdditionally, we have a rapid change of the slope of the tangent line when we pass through $x=-1$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=-1,$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/1ca0e089b095330fb1441325c2a71a56423df60c6cb18acade6d75f06a040ef1.png)",
